# Per-lab capability factory

Parent: [[Designs/Lab Capsule and Factory Design Plan]]. Extends [[Designs/Capability Capsule Factory Feature]]. Each lab's AI4R owns an independent factory, job state, templates, build inputs, candidate catalog and Docker images. Factories can evolve with their lab. A shared registry exchanges artifacts rather than replacing local factories.

## Workflow and responsibility

| Stage | Output | Boundary/failure |
|---|---|---|
| Request/gap | Bounded capability task, parent and acceptance obligations | Separate authoring journey; a running research plan records a gap rather than rewriting itself |
| Authorized preparation | Pinned paper/repository/data snapshots and provenance/access/license findings | Current M1 intake cloning and Builder network restrictions remain; a new preparation policy needs native reconciliation |
| Reference and operation selection | Concrete operations, upstream reference calls and environment evidence | Paper-only work may yield a resource; unavailable reference evidence remains visible |
| Authoring | Thin implementation, declaration, recipe, locks, claimed tests and failure modes | Author cannot replace the independent checks assigned to judge it |
| Local build | Image digest/platform and record tied to exact context/source | Factory uses the local build broker; capsules receive no general Docker control; failed builds retain diagnostics |
| Independent qualification | Conformance, source/reference fidelity, real invocation and resource/effect checks | Input-dependent fidelity stays in protected host checks; semantic verifier remains declaration/output-only |
| Package and local admission | Candidate source/image/evidence manifest, then a separate eligibility decision | Protected local owner decides future-plan eligibility; built/stored/exported does not mean admitted |
| Export and central qualification | Source delta, recipe/dependencies, image reference/archive and evidence | Central owner rebuilds/requalifies and decides adoption; candidate upload never updates the tested base |

Missing rights, data, models or dependencies blocks the affected output or narrows the declared scope. Packaging success proves neither whole-repository correctness nor scientific validity.

## Jobs, bootstrap and evolution

Preserve stage identities/evidence across interruptions. Resume only from verified artifacts under matching pins; changed sources, recipes or obligations invalidate dependent stages or create a new attempt. Repair is a separately budgeted development attempt retaining failures, not an invisible retry of a research node. Headless jobs record blocked outcomes rather than waiting indefinitely for human input.

Bootstrap each VM with a compatible tested AI4R/factory and seed CC images. The factory builds with existing capabilities plus controlled infrastructure services; it cannot depend on its not-yet-built output to bootstrap itself. Factory stages can later become reusable CCs. Begin with a flat governed workflow. Composite execution requires its own pinned members, typed links and member-level enforcement; Docker packaging does not supply those semantics.

Labs can improve CCs, factory templates/stages, planner/workflows and AI4R implementation. Keep each change in its native form; ordinary code/config need not become a CC. Factory/platform changes are lab release candidates evaluated against fixed incumbent checks before installation for new jobs. In-flight work retains its compatible pinned environment; stop and record incompatibility if that environment cannot be retained.

## Contributions and divergence

A CC-only candidate may be adopted independently if it fits the target base protocol/resources. Declare runner/factory/planner dependencies explicitly. Platform changes use a separate patch/release path. Sibling candidates from two labs retain lineage and are compared against the same central baseline. Requalify a merged result; timestamps and local version numbers do not decide which is better.

Central adoption publishes compatible source/image/admission/migration pins. Labs explicitly import or update; they never follow a floating tag mid-project. Retain deprecated pins needed for evidence and replay, with a separate revocation policy for future execution. Identify inaccessible resources and redistribution limits in exports; qualification may require an authorized environment rather than copying private data.

## First demonstration and native reconciliation

Choose one repeatable operation from an authorized prepared source. A builds, qualifies and locally admits its image; a new plan invokes it. B reconstructs/imports and independently qualifies it while continuing its unrelated project. Demonstrate bad-candidate rejection, interrupted build recovery, missing dependency, explicit export and a separate central adoption decision. Repeat with a factory/platform change to demonstrate broader evolution.

Affected existing owners: M1-001 (adapter/runtime qualification), M1-002 (library, binding, runner, checks/evidence/admission), M1-003/004/005 (intent, profiles and planning), and M1-008 where bounded RSI is affected. These are current ownership references, not new task assignments. Current manual activation and M1 plan restrictions remain until amended. No factory or multi-VM result is claimed here.
